      "abstract": "In this thesis, I explore Michel Foucault's early and later works on Immanuel Kant. I argue that the influence of Kant on Foucault deserves further examination. The thesis is divided into three parts. In Part I, which comprises the first two chapters, I will examine relevant aspects of Foucault scholarship that deal primarily with Foucault's work on Kant. In Part II, I will examine Foucault's Introduction to Kant's Anthropology to demonstrate that Foucault's critique of modernity is incomplete without Kant. In Chapter III, I will show how Foucault's critique of modernity is rooted in Kant. In Chapter IV, I will demonstrate that, even though he inaugurated modernity, and hence may be subjected to the same criticism as modernity, nonetheless Kant, for Foucault, gives a lesson that rectifies modernity's mistake. In Part III, I will examine Foucault's later work on Kant, particularly the essay, 'What is Enlightenment?', to demonstrate how Kant enables Foucault to interrogate the present and ascertain the form of this interrogation. In Chapter V, I will show that, even though he rejects Kant's ideas of the present such as epoch and event, nonetheless Foucault appropriates Kant\u2019s notion of 'Enlightenment' in his consideration of the present. In Chapter VI, I will argue that Kant exhibits the 'attitude of modernity', thereby assisting Foucault in determining the 'modern' form of critique. Drawing all this together, I conclude that Kant is significant for Foucault because he enables Foucault to undertake a critique of modernity and at the same time determine that to be critical is to be modern",
